"""Forty-two original Yuktobanian continuations; no new canonical institutions."""
from pacific_nations.model import Branch

TAG='YUK';NAME='Yuktobania'
OPENING_TITLE='The Names beneath the Totals'
OPENING="""The allocation return balances before anyone enters the room. A receiving secretary has nevertheless left a column unfinished. Beside the totals she wants the names of the offices expected to answer for them. The command liaison calls this unnecessary detail until a workshop representative asks him who can amend a reservation when its original recipient is no longer available. He cannot find the answer in the balanced columns. The hearing begins there. It will retain the government's inherited intervention and command choices, but ask a more immediate question: who is allowed to say that the work requested on paper has not reached the people expected to receive it?"""
CONCLUSION_TITLE='A Return with Somewhere to Go'
CONCLUSION="""The national return is balanced again. This time, the receiving secretary can follow each reservation to an office, a signed instruction and an account of the work completed. There are still unanswered requests. A commander wants another protected period; a civil representative wants a waiting workshop released from an old promise. Neither has been erased to make the conclusion look unanimous. The government can keep an open receiving record or a bounded professional account. The choice will matter when the next demand arrives, whether a partner has answered, refused or ceased to be eligible. The return has become useful because somebody can finally be asked to receive it."""
FINAL_REQUIREMENT='has_country_flag = YUK_snc_field_answers_review_done has_country_flag = YUK_snc_readiness_truth_review_done'
PUBLIC_TRIGGERS={
 'ac_snc_yuk_verification_earned':'has_country_flag = YUK_snc_field_answers_settlement_done OR = { AND = { has_idea = yuk_reformed_field_command has_completed_focus = YUK_army_reform_complete check_variable = { ac_snp_yuk_civil >= 60 } has_country_flag = YUK_snc_field_answers_civil_done has_country_flag = ac_snc_yuk_message_10_public } AND = { has_idea = yuk_expeditionary_command has_country_flag = YUK_exp_command_assessment_done check_variable = { ac_snp_yuk_civil >= 40 } has_country_flag = YUK_snc_field_answers_protected_done has_country_flag = YUK_snc_field_answers_staff_return_done has_country_flag = ac_snc_yuk_message_20_staff } }',
 'ac_snc_yuk_channel_earned':'has_country_flag = YUK_snc_scientific_custody_settlement_done',
 'ac_snc_yuk_science_channel_earned':'has_country_flag = YUK_snc_scientific_custody_settlement_done has_country_flag = YUK_snp_technical_cooperation_service_completed has_country_flag = ac_yuk_arkbird_partner has_global_flag = ac_sp_arkbird_complete country_exists = OSE OSE = { original_tag = OSE has_country_flag = OSE_snc_pacific_channel_settlement_done }',
}

BRANCHES=(
Branch(TAG,'field_answers','training','2006.1.1',('command','communications'),(
 ('Ask Where the Order Arrived',"""An instruction has reached the field with every required signature and none of the explanation its receiving officer needs. The staff secretary can trace its approval without finding anybody authorized to answer the resulting question. Begin from the command structure the government actually retained. A delegated service and an expeditionary staff will not give the same answer, and this review will not pretend they have chosen the same history. Both must identify who receives an objection, who may clarify an instruction, and which uncertainty the next officer is expected to carry rather than quietly remove."""),
 ('Give the Question a Hearing',"""The civil receiving representative asks that a question from the field be heard before it is classified as a failure to obey. The command liaison agrees that an honest uncertainty can improve an instruction, but warns that answering it will take political attention and trained staff. Accept that burden. The resulting procedure supports professional cohesion through an account that people can challenge. It does not rewrite the government's inherited command reform or its earlier intervention decision. It gives the existing structure a clearer way to distinguish an officer asking for missing information from an officer refusing a responsibility already understood."""),
 ('Require a Staff Countercheck',"""The staff propose keeping a protected countercheck within the professional chain. A question would reach a named officer who must either answer it or sign the reason it remains unanswered. Civil services will bear the opportunity cost of the protected schedule. An expeditionary command can adopt that responsibility without pretending to have selected delegated field reform. Its credibility depends on preserving the objection beside the final instruction, including when a more convenient account would describe the matter as settled. A protected answer must still be an answer somebody is prepared to receive and defend."""),
 ('Follow a Fresh Instruction',"""Request a new funded training period to follow an instruction through the selected receiving procedure. Earlier exercises explain why the present command chain exists, but cannot certify the work it is doing now. The corresponding civil or protected lane must complete its actual period after this request. Staff will retain the first question, the authority that received it and the response that reached the next officer. If the period is interrupted, the missing return stays missing until a current request and genuine new work provide an answer that belongs to the present instruction."""),
 ('Return to the First Question',"""The completed period ends with the first question still attached to its answer. A reviewing officer says the pair makes the instruction seem less decisive. The receiving secretary says it makes the responsibility easier to find. Review the actual training work and the burden accepted by its selected lane. A cancelled period cannot be counted merely because the old completion stamp remains in the archive. The useful finding is whether an officer could ask for clarification without disappearing from the account, and whether the person who answered could explain the authority used to do so."""),
 ('Keep the Public Objection',"""Retain the objection in an open receiving account. A public reader will not see every protected operational detail, but can recognize which question changed the government's explanation and which one did not. Maintaining that distinction costs political effort. It can also strengthen confidence in a command that is willing to distinguish scrutiny from disloyalty. The choice does not erase the earlier staff structure or promise that another government will respond in kind. It leaves a usable record for the next person who discovers that an instruction has travelled farther than the explanation accompanying it."""),
 ('Keep the Signed Correction',"""The professional return keeps the correction beside the original instruction, signed by the staff officer responsible for receiving both. Its defenders accept that restricted circulation creates a continuing duty to preserve the disagreement, rather than a convenient reason to forget it. Adopt the protected record with its civilian burden. An expeditionary command can earn this countercheck through real work and a later assessment, while retaining the civil receiving margin that other obligations still require. It is a distinct route from delegated reform, not a different title pasted over the same assumed decision."""),
 ('An Order That Can Be Answered',"""The standing receiving procedure can now identify the officer allowed to answer a question and the account in which that answer will remain. Retain the chosen civil or protected policy, improving its practice through the completed review without removing its cost. The inherited intervention and command records remain their own responsibilities. A later diplomatic effort may read this earned verification account, but the procedure is useful even when no foreign partner accepts an exchange. Its immediate achievement is that the person expected to act can find the limit of the instruction and somebody responsible for explaining it."""),
),(
 ('Civil hearing for field questions',{'army_org_factor':.015},{'political_power_gain':-.035}),
 ('Protected staff countercheck',{'army_org_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The receiving secretary places the first instruction beside its last approved copy. The wording is the same; the accompanying explanation is not. A liaison officer begins describing the inherited command settlement, then stops when the field representative asks which desk would have accepted his original question. They are not being invited to choose their past again. They can open a civil hearing or retain a signed professional countercheck within the structure they have. Either account must preserve the place where somebody was expected to act without knowing which part of the instruction another office considered settled.\n\n[ac_snp_yuk_intervention_context]\n[ac_snp_yuk_command_context]""",
"""The field representative recognizes his question in the completed return. Beside it is the receiving officer's answer and a qualification neither of them had expected to need. 'I can use this,' he says. The liaison asks whether it should become an open account or a signed professional correction. The secretary records the distinction. The chosen audience will change the burden of explaining the result, but it must not change which command structure performed the work. A useful review has found a receiving responsibility; it has not quietly exchanged the government's earlier decisions for a more convenient history.\n\n[ac_snp_yuk_command_context]"""),

Branch(TAG,'readiness_truth','industrial_orders','2006.1.1',('industry','tools'),(
 ('Readiness beside the Workshop',"""The readiness return and the workshop schedule describe the same technical staff as available to different recipients. Neither office has counted the time needed to hand work over. Open a review that places the two accounts together, beginning from the existing industrial-orders settlement. The government must decide which civilian training and replacement duties receive protection, and what the other claimants will be told to postpone. An accurate readiness account should explain the work that can actually be sustained, rather than the amount of demand that an office was willing to approve on somebody else's behalf."""),
 ('Protect Civil Technical Time',"""Reserve technical time for civilian maintenance and instruction before additional urgent orders acquire it by default. The choice supports workshop continuity, while requiring political attention when protected claimants ask why their own work must wait. Training representatives agree to name the release periods clearly enough that the command can plan around them. The policy does not promise a second workforce. It makes the existing allocation more dependable by allowing a receiving office to say that people who are already answering one responsibility cannot be counted as an uncommitted reserve for another."""),
 ('Protect the Replacement Return',"""The command receiving office asks for a protected replacement return, with technical supervision available when an order reaches the workshop. Predictability can improve the use of existing production, but civilian services will bear the capacity withheld from their own work. Accept that continuing burden openly. A completed requisition is not a completed replacement, and the receiving office remains responsible for the difference. The policy protects a bounded schedule rather than promising equipment that has not been manufactured. Its account must retain the names of the civilian claimants whose waiting time helped make that schedule dependable."""),
 ('Inspect a New Receiving Period',"""Open a fresh industrial-orders review through the existing selected-lane funded service. The committee wants the present receiving schedule, not another copy of an earlier inspection that happened to finish successfully. Keep the technical release, replacement request and acceptance account together while the work proceeds. The actual period must complete after this request before a review can be credited. An interruption will leave its spent time in the record without supplying a conclusion. The government must then decide whether to reopen a current request and perform the corresponding work again."""),
 ('Compare the Available Hands',"""The completed receiving period records a delay that neither opening account predicted. Technical staff spent time teaching a replacement examiner how to repeat an acceptance check. The workshop regards that as useful work; the first readiness summary did not count it. Review the actual return before deciding which explanation becomes standing practice. The selected policy must answer for its own burden as well as its advantage. A neat total is inadequate if the same qualified people have again been promised to two receiving offices without either being told where they are expected to appear first."""),
 ('Publish the Receiving Margin',"""The public account will identify the receiving margin the government actually protected, including time spent on instruction and examination. Its political cost comes from explaining why an apparently idle interval was not available for another urgent demand. Workshop representatives accept the scrutiny because a visible margin is harder to spend twice without an argument. The record does not claim universal approval. It provides a civil reference that another receiving office can use when an approved order arrives with an assumption that the qualified people needed to complete it must therefore be waiting somewhere nearby."""),
 ('Preserve the Technical Return',"""Retain a signed technical return that a later receiving officer can repeat. Supervisors want the teaching interval and the delayed examination left beside the corrected schedule, even though a shorter report would make the period appear more efficient. The protected account supports professional continuity while retaining its civilian opportunity cost. It cannot claim that every displaced service accepted the priority. Its value is more practical: the next examiner can see how the workshop discovered a limit, which responsibility was changed, and why the additional signature represented work rather than another way to avoid receiving it."""),
 ('A Readiness Return That Holds',"""The standing return identifies what the selected receiving schedule can sustain and what it leaves outside its boundary. Improve the chosen civil or protected practice through the completed review, keeping the same continuing cost. The workshop remains responsible for actual production and examination; a policy cannot stand in for either. If another country refuses a technical exchange or the surrounding security conditions change, the domestic receiving method still works. It gives the government a way to retain useful readiness without asking its technical staff to make a balanced allocation look like an unlimited supply of available hands."""),
),(
 ('Civil technical release periods',{'production_factory_efficiency_gain_factor':.025},{'political_power_gain':-.03}),
 ('Protected replacement examination',{'production_factory_max_efficiency_factor':.015},{'consumer_goods_factor':.005}),
),
"""The superintendent asks the committee to count the people standing behind the completed order. The receiving officer replies that the return already contains a total. She opens the teaching schedule and shows him where the same examiner appears twice. 'Which office receives her first?' The question changes the hearing. They can publish a civil account of the actual margin or keep a signed professional return detailed enough to repeat the examination. Neither route can remove the time she spent preparing someone else to do the work safely, however much cleaner the totals would look without it.""",
"""The new examiner can explain why one order waited, which was the purpose of keeping him beside the work rather than only beside its paperwork. The superintendent calls that a successful receiving period. The command liaison agrees, then points to the claimants who carried the delay. The final account must retain both. A public margin will require political explanation; a protected technical return will continue reserving civilian capacity. The government is choosing how to preserve a useful practice, not deciding that the work it displaced has become somebody else's problem because the scheduled period is complete."""),

Branch(TAG,'ports_orders','maritime_support','2006.1.1',('navy','logistics'),(
 ('Two Orders for One Window',"""The port receiving office has two approved orders for the same maintenance window. One concerns a civilian delivery; the other concerns protected naval support. Both arrived with a note describing the request as already coordinated. Begin from the existing maritime-support settlement and ask who can resolve the collision without hiding the losing claimant. The review concerns receiving work that can be performed under the present national allocation. It does not require a foreign port, an available overseas partner or a particular ship to remain in service for the office to explain which responsibility it is prepared to sustain."""),
 ('Keep the Civil Port Window',"""Give civil receiving work a dependable port window and require protected services to name the requests they will postpone around it. This improves maintenance continuity while placing a readiness burden on the schedule. The receiving officer must explain the limit before another urgent order assumes the window has quietly become available. A civil priority is not a claim that security work is unnecessary. It is a way to retain ordinary transport and repair responsibilities when those responsibilities have repeatedly been described as flexible by offices that did not have to receive the delayed work themselves."""),
 ('Keep the Fleet Support Window',"""Protect a bounded naval-support window with the technical staff and receiving authority needed to use it. The policy improves the dependability of existing support while retaining the civilian capacity cost it imposes. An officer who wants to amend the order must identify the claimant displaced, rather than send the receiving office a new note saying that coordination has already occurred. The account makes no promise about an individual vessel's availability. It describes a national support responsibility that can be sustained and reviewed even when an overseas arrangement has been refused or interrupted."""),
 ('Request a Fresh Port Return',"""Request a new funded maritime-support period through the selected national lane. Its receiving work begins from the actual domestic responsibility, so a foreign refusal cannot prevent the review from being completed. The committee will retain both original orders and the instruction used to resolve their collision. Earlier port returns remain useful background, but do not certify the present schedule. The corresponding service must genuinely complete after this request. If the work is interrupted, the renewed review will use a current date and a new receiving period rather than an explanation copied from the old one."""),
 ('Hear the Displaced Claimant',"""Invite the displaced claimant back with the completed receiving report. The office that obtained its window must explain not only how the selected work proceeded, but how the other service learned that its approved order would wait. Review the actual funded period against the policy that authorized it. A completed return may show that the national priority worked while its explanation failed. That distinction belongs in the assessment. The government has not earned a standing port procedure if its dependability still rests on somebody else discovering the changed timetable only after arriving to receive the promised work."""),
 ('Open the Port Account',"""The civil representative wants the changed timetable and the receiving explanation left in an open account. It will take political work to defend a priority to people who can identify their own delayed request in the record. Accept that burden in exchange for a schedule whose limits can be challenged before a claimant acts on the wrong assumption. Publication does not reveal protected technical procedures or promise an overseas agreement. It establishes who can answer when two approved orders arrive for one window, and prevents successful completion from quietly removing the losing claimant from the national story."""),
 ('Retain the Signed Timetable',"""Keep a signed professional timetable with both orders and the instruction that changed their receiving sequence. The protected route supports dependable staff work while preserving its civilian opportunity cost. It must retain the explanation given to the delayed claimant, including when that explanation was late or inadequate. A useful professional account should help the next officer recognize a collision before it reaches the port. It should not become a shorter version of events in which everybody appears to have agreed merely because the service that received its window completed the work it was finally allowed to perform."""),
 ('A Port Return with a Recipient',"""The standing procedure names an actual recipient for an amended order. Retain and improve the selected civil or protected policy through its completed review, without removing the accepted cost. Domestic port and maintenance work remains useful when cooperation abroad fails, a partner becomes ineligible, or a wider conflict makes the hoped-for arrangement impossible. The office can still identify its current responsibilities and answer for the order it changed. The final return leaves the delayed request attached, so a successor will inherit both the useful schedule and the person who still has a reason to question it."""),
),(
 ('Civil port receiving window',{'supply_consumption_factor':-.02},{'army_org_factor':-.005}),
 ('Protected naval support window',{'supply_consumption_factor':-.025},{'consumer_goods_factor':.005}),
),
"""The civilian receiver has brought the order that told him his window was confirmed. The naval liaison has brought an equally confident instruction. The port secretary places them together without deciding which uniform should speak first. 'Somebody amended a schedule neither of you received,' she says. They can make the explanation public or retain a signed professional account, but both versions must keep the delayed claimant in view. There is no foreign delegation whose approval can resolve this particular collision. The government must decide how its own receiving offices will answer before it offers a wider arrangement to anyone else.""",
"""The selected period is complete, and the delayed receiver knows who amended his order. He still believes the priority should have been different. The port secretary records his objection beside the liaison's explanation rather than averaging them into agreement. A public account will make the choice easier to challenge; a professional return will retain more detail within the receiving chain. Either route carries a continuing burden. The useful change is that a future claimant can find a responsible recipient before bringing an approved order to a window that has already been promised to somebody else."""),

Branch(TAG,'scientific_custody','technical_cooperation','2006.1.1',('science','satellite'),(
 ('Where the Method Was Received',"""A technical method has reached the research office with a covering letter that describes a broader understanding than the attached record can establish. The scientists do not dispute the value of the received work. They dispute the additional promise made in its name. Begin from the existing technical-cooperation settlement and trace which methods were examined domestically, which arrived through an established partnership, and which questions remain unanswered. A completed debris-clearing programme is part of that history. It does not authorize a receiving officer to invent a new capability, an operating commitment or another government's continuing consent."""),
 ('Publish the Civil Method',"""Adopt a civil method whose stated limitations travel with the result. The research office must explain what the examination established and which questions it could not answer. Defending that precision takes political attention when a broader announcement would have sounded more impressive. In return, the method remains useful to domestic researchers without a foreign acceptance. Cooperation can add evidence where its existing record supports it, but an unanswered inquiry remains unanswered. A published account should be clear enough that a later receiving office can use the result without inheriting a promise the scientists never made."""),
 ('Keep a Protected Examination',"""A protected examination can retain technical detail that cannot be circulated openly, provided its receiving authority and limitations remain signed. Reserve the necessary civilian capacity and accept the resulting opportunity cost. The professional route must preserve the unanswered questions as carefully as the useful findings. It cannot treat an old partnership record as permanent permission to answer for another country, or a completed debris programme as an operating military system. If an exchange is refused or interrupted, the domestic examination continues from the material it actually received and states where the missing answer would have mattered."""),
 ('Request a New Custody Review',"""Request a fresh technical-cooperation period through the existing selected-lane service. Start with domestic methods that the receiving office can actually examine. The work may use an established cooperative record where relevant, but does not depend on a new foreign acceptance. The corresponding funded period must genuinely finish after this request. Its return will identify the method, the authority that received it and the question it was allowed to answer. An earlier successful examination cannot certify the present chain merely because its completed stamp is easier to find than the people currently expected to sign."""),
 ('Separate the Three Accounts',"""The completed review separates domestic examination, received cooperative material and unanswered inquiry. A liaison asks whether the three can be summarized together. The researcher says they can be described together, but should not become the same kind of evidence. Review the actual funded period while retaining that distinction. The selected policy must explain its political or civilian burden as well as the usefulness of the method. If a partnership is absent or no longer eligible, the domestic account still provides a conclusion. It does not need to fill the empty space in the cooperative record with an invented agreement."""),
 ('Release the Limited Result',"""Publish the result together with its limit. The receiving staff expect questions about why an answer stops where a previous announcement seemed to continue. They accept the political burden because a method that can be challenged is less likely to be mistaken for an unqualified promise. The account leaves the received cooperative material in its proper place and preserves the unanswered inquiry without treating it as a refusal that nobody actually issued. It supports a later verified exchange, if eligible participants choose one, while remaining a complete domestic practice when no such exchange can be arranged."""),
 ('Keep the Signed Custody Record',"""Retain a professional custody record that follows the actual method and its examination, with a named receiving authority beside each handover. Its protected schedule continues to reserve civilian capacity. The staff agree that the restriction makes it more important to keep the unanswered inquiry in the file. A later officer should be able to distinguish a question that was refused from one that never reached the appropriate recipient. The record can support a new verification channel, but cannot establish another country's present consent or resurrect an earlier programme by repeating the name written on its completed return."""),
 ('A Method with an Honest Limit',"""The standing method retains its selected civil or protected explanation and the burden accepted to sustain it. Improve that practice through the completed review without claiming that every hoped-for exchange occurred. Existing project and partnership records remain authoritative for what they actually established. The domestic receiving method is useful after refusal, interruption, war or a partner's loss of eligibility. Its final account gives a successor enough information to ask a better question without first untangling an invented promise from the scientific work that was genuinely received, examined and kept for the people who will use it next."""),
),(
 ('Published scientific receiving method',{'research_speed_factor':.015},{'political_power_gain':-.03}),
 ('Protected custody examination',{'research_speed_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The researcher reads the covering letter twice. 'This part is our finding,' she says, 'and this part is somebody's hope for the next exchange.' The liaison does not deny the difference. He asks which account will be useful if no answer arrives. An open civil method could make the limit easier to question; a signed professional examination could preserve detail within a protected chain. Both must identify what was actually received. The committee is choosing how to keep an honest scientific account, not granting a new operational purpose to a programme whose earlier work has its own signed account.\n\n[ac_snp_arkbird_context]""",
"""The received method can be repeated by a researcher who did not attend the original exchange. That is the strongest finding in the completed review. The unanswered question remains beside it. 'We do not need to disguise the empty space,' the receiving secretary says. The committee can publish the limit or retain a signed custody record, with the continuing cost of either choice. Cooperation may support a later dialogue, but the result does not depend on claiming another government's agreement. The domestic office has earned a method that still works when the useful answer is smaller than the invitation that carried it.\n\n[ac_snp_arkbird_context]"""),

Branch(TAG,'civil_receiving','distribution_review','2010.1.1',('civil','logistics'),(
 ('The Request behind the Priority',"""A protected priority has been renewed three times while the civil receiving office keeps the same unanswered request at the bottom of its schedule. The command liaison says the situation remains difficult. The secretary agrees, then asks whether that difficulty explains the priority or has become a substitute for answering the request. Review the present distribution responsibility from the existing national settlement. The work remains useful in peace, external danger or prolonged warfare. It asks which offices receive current needs, who can amend an allocation, and how the people waiting will learn that their claim has actually been heard."""),
 ('Retain the Civil Receiving Floor',"""Protect a civil receiving floor inside the existing allocation, rather than ask ordinary services to survive on whatever the protected schedule leaves unspecified. The policy improves repair continuity while reducing the readiness margin available elsewhere. Its representatives must identify the work that belongs within the floor and answer when another claimant challenges that description. The government is not creating a second pool of capacity. It is making a priority visible enough that a later emergency request cannot silently consume it while leaving the receiving office responsible for a promise it no longer has the means to keep."""),
 ('Keep a Bounded Command Claim',"""The command liaison proposes retaining a bounded priority whose receiving office must explain each renewal. Protect that claim while keeping the civilian opportunity cost beside it. The choice can improve professional readiness, but cannot turn every difficult condition into an instruction to postpone the same ordinary services indefinitely. A claimant who receives no window still deserves an answer that identifies the authority and the reason. The government's inherited intervention and command structure remain in force. This new policy concerns how their present obligations are received, not how their earlier choices should be remembered differently."""),
 ('Request the Current Distribution',"""Open a fresh funded distribution-review period through the selected existing lane. The committee wants the allocation being used now, including the current claimant left outside its priority. Earlier completed periods explain how the schedule developed, but cannot provide the missing present answer. The actual service must complete after this request. During war it may deliver reduced domestic continuity and must be understood on those terms. If the receiving period is interrupted, record the spent work without crediting a conclusion and require a current request before a new period can supply the account."""),
 ('Hear the Waiting Service',"""The waiting service has finally received an explanation it can challenge. It has not necessarily received the window it wanted. Review the completed funded period without confusing those outcomes. A selected civil floor can leave a protected demand unanswered; a bounded command claim can leave an ordinary repair delayed. Both costs belong in the receiving account. The committee should be able to identify who answered, which authority was used and whether the claimant learned the changed condition before acting on the old promise. A balanced allocation alone cannot tell that part of the story."""),
 ('A Priority That Must Answer',"""Retain the selected civil or command priority as standing receiving practice, improved by the completed review and accompanied by its continuing burden. The office can use that method even when a foreign partner refuses, a hoped-for arrangement becomes impossible or the security situation remains unresolved. Its conclusion is about how the government answers its current obligations. It does not require a national victory, a historical deadline or another country's acceptance to become useful. A successor will inherit the reason for the priority, the name of its recipient and the waiting request that the completed account deliberately kept in view."""),
),(
 ('Civil receiving floor',{'repair_speed_factor':.02},{'army_org_factor':-.006}),
 ('Bounded command receiving claim',{'army_org_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The waiting representative reads the third renewal aloud. The wording is almost identical to the first. The command liaison begins to explain why the conditions remain difficult, then looks at the date of the unanswered request. 'You have heard the priority,' the secretary says. 'We are asking who received the claim behind it.' A civil floor and a bounded command claim would impose different burdens, but either needs a responsible receiving office. The hearing can preserve an open account or a signed professional return. It cannot turn the claimant's continued patience into evidence that the explanation was adequate.""",
"""The completed distribution period has not given every claimant the requested window. It has given the waiting service a recipient able to explain which obligation took priority. The representative still disagrees, and the liaison leaves that disagreement in the return. 'At least the next request has somewhere to go,' the secretary says. The committee must decide how to retain the account and its continuing cost. The conclusion is useful whether the external situation improved or became harder. It concerns the government's ability to receive an obligation without first requiring the person presenting it to disappear from the schedule."""),

Branch(TAG,'national_return','orders_review','2011.1.1',('document','assembly'),(
 ('Place the Returns Together',"""Bring the field-answer and readiness reviews together only after both have actual completed returns. The receiving secretary does not ask their officers to agree about every national priority. She asks them to identify where an instruction, a qualified person and an accountable recipient finally met. The earlier command and intervention records remain attached. A concluding account must be useful if a partner refused, disappeared or became ineligible, and if the surrounding conflict has not ended. It concerns what the government can hand to a successor without disguising an unanswered request as work that somebody else must already have received."""),
 ('Leave an Open Receiving Account',"""Keep the concluding receiving account open to civil challenge. The selected practice improves ordinary repair continuity while requiring continued political attention to defend the priorities it preserves. The government does not have to describe every claimant as satisfied. It has to retain enough of the actual decisions that a successor can understand why a request was postponed and who remained responsible for answering it. Earlier reviews provide the earned work behind this conclusion. There is no reason to invent another period merely to make the final return look newer than the obligations it is intended to carry forward."""),
 ('Leave a Signed Professional Account',"""Retain a signed professional account with the authority and technical responsibility needed to repeat its receiving method. The protected practice improves command continuity while keeping the civilian cost of its reserved attention. It must preserve the field questions and the workshop margin that earlier reviews uncovered, rather than reduce them to a favourable total. The government is not choosing its inherited command structure again or promising another country's cooperation. It is choosing the account a successor will receive when the people who remember the original disagreement are no longer sitting beside every desk that depends on its answer."""),
 ('A Return Someone Will Receive',"""Keep the chosen civil or protected national account as a standing practice, improved through the reviews that genuinely earned it. The continuing cost remains. The receiving secretary leaves room for a successor's correction, the command liaison keeps the unanswered field question attached, and the workshop retains the explanation of its actual margin. The account can be completed when its real obligations have been answered, even if the expected timetable has long passed. It makes no claim that a conflict ended or a foreign exchange succeeded. It gives the next receiving office something more dependable than a balanced total: the responsibilities the government accepted, the work it completed and the limits it was willing to preserve."""),
),(
 ('Open national receiving account',{'repair_speed_factor':.02},{'political_power_gain':-.035}),
 ('Signed national professional account',{'army_org_factor':.02},{'consumer_goods_factor':.004}),
),
"""The command liaison and the workshop representative arrive with different folders and recognize several of the same unanswered questions. The secretary asks them to keep both copies. 'The next office should know why you disagreed,' she says. They can leave an open receiving account or a signed professional return, each with a continuing burden. Neither route allows the final total to replace the actual field and readiness reviews. The choice concerns how to hand on a useful national responsibility without requiring a successor to recreate every conversation before finding the person expected to answer.\n\n[ac_snp_yuk_intervention_context]\n[ac_snp_yuk_command_context]""",
"""The national return is ready for an office whose staff have not yet been appointed to receive it. That is the secretary's final test. The account names a responsibility clearly enough for somebody outside the original room to question it. The liaison keeps his signed qualification beside the workshop's objection. The committee can preserve an open or professional record, but not a version in which both have quietly become unanimous. Whether the wider world has answered or left another inquiry waiting, the government has a useful account of the work it actually completed and the limits it chose to keep."""),
)
